Subject: [PATCH] server: a reply sent back is kept as it was generated

A chat client sends the previous reply back as a message. The chat template
writes that message out again and the text is tokenised again, and the result
is not always the tokens the slot holds:

- The same text can be spelled with other tokens. The model wrote " book" and
  "marks", the tokeniser gives " bookmarks"; "<parameter" "=" "path" against
  "=path".
- The whitespace around the end of the reasoning is the template's. It trims
  the reasoning and writes its own separator before the end-of-reasoning
  marker; the reply parser drops the whitespace after the marker from the
  content, and the template writes its own there too. When a reasoning budget
  ends the thinking in the middle of a line, the model's whitespace on either
  side is not what the template writes.

The prompt cache then stops matching inside the reply. A recurrent state
cannot go back a few tokens, so the slot returns to its context checkpoint and
reads the whole reply again; resumed from a parked save (patches/0016) it has
no checkpoint in the last firing, goes back to the save before, and reads the
whole firing. A parked key is then rewritten from there instead of appended
to. Seen on 10 of 227 follow-up turns of Qwen3.8-27B served to agents, 84 to
10,508 tokens read again each time.

When a task is handed to its slot (launch_slot_with_task) and the slot's
tokens stop matching the prompt, the two are compared from there as text: the
same marker tokens (control and user-defined, by id) in the same order and
the same bytes between them. Up to the furthest pair of token boundaries
where they still say the same, the prompt takes the slot's tokens; the
message spans move with them. From there the existing path decides as
before, on a prompt that now shares that much more with the slot.

- One place is excepted: the end-of-reasoning marker that closes the thinking
  block the template opened in an assistant turn (the first one after an
  assistant delimiter followed, with nothing but whitespace between, by the
  start tag). The whitespace right before it and the leading whitespace of
  the text right after it are not compared: no client can send back what the
  model wrote there. At any other end-of-reasoning marker, one the model
  wrote into its answer or one in a user or a tool message, everything is
  compared. The task keeps the delimiters its spans were found with
  (server-task.h) so that the turns can be found in the slot's tokens too.
  A raw prompt has no template: nothing is excepted there.
- Whitespace that is not compared is only passed whole and together with
  what it belongs to, so a prompt never takes the whitespace of both sides or
  of neither. The prompt that results is compared once more, whole, with the
  prompt that came in: if they do not say the same it is not used (a warning
  in the log; LLAMA_REPLY_AS_GENERATED_TEST_REJECT forces that, for its test).
- The stretch need not reach the end of either. A later message that was
  really changed, a reply that max_tokens cut off (the slot holds a reply
  less its last token) or that was cancelled, stop it where they differ, and
  the slot goes back to a checkpoint from there, not from the kept reply.
- Once a reply is kept, every later prompt renders it again and stops
  matching at the same token, so the comparison runs on every request of that
  conversation, over the tokens from that reply on (17 ms at 154K tokens).
- Completion tasks with cache_prompt only; not a child task, a decision, an
  alora, or a slot or prompt with media.
- prompt_tokens in the usage then counts the kept tokens, a token or two away
  from what tokenising the rendered prompt gives.
- Off unless LLAMA_REPLY_AS_GENERATED is 1, true, on or yes; the fleet controller sets it for its hosts.
